=== JSON Type CRUD Demo (CUBRID 10.2+) ===

[setup] Created cookbook_user_prefs with JSON column
[1] Inserted 3 JSON documents

[2] All rows (raw JSON):
    user_id=1  email=alice@example.com
    preferences={"theme":"dark","newsletter":true,"tags":["python","cubrid"]}
    user_id=2  email=bob@example.com
    preferences={"theme":"light","newsletter":false,"tags":["java"]}
    user_id=3  email=carol@example.com
    preferences={"theme":"dark","newsletter":true,"tags":["python","rust","go"]}

[3] JSON_EXTRACT('$.theme') — RAW (note the double quotes):
    user_id=1  theme_raw='"dark"'
    user_id=2  theme_raw='"light"'
    user_id=3  theme_raw='"dark"'

[4] JSON_UNQUOTE(JSON_EXTRACT(...)) — clean scalar value:
    user_id=1  theme='dark'
    user_id=2  theme='light'
    user_id=3  theme='dark'

[5] Users with theme='dark':
    user_id=1  email=alice@example.com
    user_id=3  email=carol@example.com

[6] Tag count per user (JSON_LENGTH on '$.tags'):
    user_id=1  tag_count=2
    user_id=2  tag_count=1
    user_id=3  tag_count=3

[7] After UPDATE: user_id=1 theme is now '"light"'
[8] After DELETE (newsletter=false): 2 rows remain

--- JSON function cheat sheet ---
  JSON_EXTRACT(doc, '$.path')            raw value (JSON-typed)
  JSON_UNQUOTE(JSON_EXTRACT(doc, '$.x')) scalar value (no quotes)
  JSON_LENGTH(doc_or_array)              element count
  JSON_REPLACE(doc, '$.k', new_value)    update existing key
  JSON_INSERT(doc, '$.k', value)         add key if absent
  JSON_REMOVE(doc, '$.k')                delete key

Path syntax:  $.key        object key
              $.key[0]     array index
              $.key.sub    nested key

CUBRID 11.2+ shorthand operators:
    doc->'$.key'   equivalent to JSON_EXTRACT (JSON-typed)
    doc->>'$.key'  equivalent to JSON_UNQUOTE(JSON_EXTRACT(...))  (scalar)
